From c9556b42deb5b0351f088476ee5713993d888628 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 18:33:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: friends, per-section sharing and For Trade lists

Users of one server can add each other as friends (by username or invite code, with accept/decline), choose for the
collection, the wishlist and the For Trade list on their own whether each is private, friends-only or public, mark
individual copies of a collection row For Trade, and compare two users: their For Trade cards that are on your wishlist,
and yours that are on theirs.

Everything starts private. Who may see what is decided in one place (services/friends.py) and checked by the server on
every request; the router and the older endpoints that show one user to another all ask it. A person who may not see
something gets 403 if they are a friend and 404 otherwise, so a private account looks like a missing one. What was paid,
price alerts, photos and custom cards never leave their owner.

The older endpoints that let any signed-in user read another user's whole collection now follow the same setting:
GET /api/collection/user/{id}, and the social leaderboard, compare and achievements routes. What someone paid and when
they added a card are left out of the first for everyone but the owner. The leaderboard, a comparison and another person's
achievements never carry an invested amount or a profit or loss (the Investor badge says whether someone is in profit, so
it is left out of another person's list), and the website's Leaderboard and Compare pages draw neither: no profit or loss
column, block or sort option. Two upstream tests that relied on the old behaviour are updated to share the collection first.

Adds three tables (friend_settings, friend_links, trade_list), created on start-up; no existing table changes.
Documented in docs/FRIENDS.md.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
