@@ -0,0 +1,125 @@
+# Friends, sharing and trading
+
+Friends lets the users of one PokéCollector server see what each other chooses to share, and find cards worth swapping.
+It needs **multi-user mode**: with it off nobody has to sign in, so nothing could be kept private, and every Friends route
+answers `403`.
+
+Nothing is shared until its owner chooses. A fresh install, an upgraded one and a brand-new user all start with everything
+private.
+
+## What a user can share
+
+Three sections, each with its own setting:
+
+| Section | What other people can see |
+|---------|---------------------------|
+| **Collection** | The cards the user owns: card, number of copies, condition, variant, language, and the card's market price from the catalogue |
+| **Wishlist** | The cards the user wants and how many |
+| **For Trade** | Only the cards the user has marked For Trade, and how many copies of each |
+
+Each section is **private** (only the user), **friends** (the user's accepted friends) or **public** (every signed-in user of
+this server, not the open internet). The three are independent: a user can share their For Trade list with friends and keep
+the rest private.
+
+What never leaves its owner, whatever the settings: what was paid for a card, what has been invested in a collection and
+what came of it (profit or loss, as an amount or a percentage, or whether someone is in profit), when a card was added,
+scan photos, product purchases and links to them, price alerts and target prices on the wishlist, custom (hand-made)
+cards, and anything about decks or binders (those have their own public-profile switches).
+
+These settings are separate from the public-profile settings under `/api/profile`. Those control the anonymous
+`/api/public` pages and are not changed by this feature.
+
+## Friends
+
+* A user finds another by **username** or **invite code**. Every user has one invite code (`K7MQ9-XWD3H`); making a new one
+  stops the old one working and does not end any friendship.
+* Asking someone sends a **friend request**. A request shares nothing. The other person **accepts** or **declines**
+  (a declined request just disappears; the asker is not told). Accepting does not share anything either: each side still
+  chooses what their friends can see.
+* If two people ask each other, they become friends at once.
+* Either person can **end** a friendship. Whatever was shared with friends stops being shared immediately.
+* A user can have at most 25 unanswered requests at a time.
+
+## For Trade
+
+A copy is For Trade only when its owner marks it, one collection row (card + variant + condition + language) at a time, and
+says how many of that row's copies are on offer. Duplicates are never marked automatically. The number others see is never
+more than the owner currently holds; if copies are removed, the mark shrinks with them and does not come back when more
+copies are added later.
+
+## Trade match
+
+`GET /api/friends/{user_id}/trade-match` compares the signed-in user with one other person:
+
+* **They have / you want** - their For Trade cards that are on your wishlist. Needs their For Trade list shared with you.
+* **You have / they want** - your For Trade cards that are on their wishlist. Needs their wishlist shared with you.
+
+A half that the other person does not share with you is empty and flagged (`can_see_their_trade_list`,
+`can_see_their_wishlist`), so the answer never hints at what is behind it. Cards match by exact catalogue id, so a German
+and an English printing are different cards.
+
+## Where privacy is enforced
+
+On the server, for every request, in `services/friends.py` (`can_view`, `owners_visible_to`, `require_view`). The router
+and the older endpoints below all ask it. A client that hides a tab it was not allowed to open gets no more than one that
+asks for it anyway. Admins get no special view through these routes.
+
+A person who may not see something is told as little as possible: a **friend** gets `403` ("ash hasn't shared their
+collection with you"); **anyone else** gets `404 User not found`, the same answer as for a user who does not exist.
+
+Responses carry `Cache-Control: private, no-store`.
+
+### Older endpoints that now follow the same settings
+
+Before this feature, any signed-in user could read any other user's whole collection. These now require the owner to share
+their collection with the viewer:
+
+| Route | Now |
+|-------|-----|
+| `GET /api/collection/user/{user_id}` | `404` unless shared with the viewer. What the owner paid and when they added each card are left out for everyone but the owner. |
+| `GET /api/social/leaderboard` | Ranks the viewer and the people who share their collection with the viewer, by what the collections are worth. A row has the username, number of cards, value at market prices, best card, sets completed and similar counts. It never has an invested amount or a profit or loss (the viewer's own row included). |
+| `GET /api/social/compare/{user_id}` | `404` unless that user's collection is shared with the viewer. Neither side carries an invested amount or a profit or loss. |
+| `GET /api/social/achievements/{user_id}` | `404` unless that user's collection is shared with the viewer (your own always works). Another person's list leaves out the Investor badge, which says whether they are in profit. |
+
+On the website this means the Leaderboard lists only people who have chosen to share, and "view collection" works only for
+them. Because the server no longer sends the invested and profit figures, the Leaderboard and Compare pages do not draw
+them either: no profit or loss column or block, and no way to sort the Leaderboard by profit. They show each trainer's
+username, avatar, number of cards, value, best card and sets completed (Compare also the different cards and what the two
+collections have in common).
+
+## API
+
+All routes are under `/api/friends` and need a signed-in user. `{user_id}` is the other person's id.
+
+| Method | Path | Notes |
+|--------|------|-------|
+| GET | `/me` | Your invite code, what you share, counts. A client uses it to find out the server has Friends. |
+| PUT | `/me/sharing` | `{"collection": "friends", "wishlist": "private", "trade": "public"}`; names only what changes. Unknown sections or levels are `422`. |
+| POST | `/me/invite-code` | New invite code; the old one stops working. |
+| GET | `/` | `friends` (with what each lets you see), `incoming` and `outgoing` requests. |
+| POST | `/requests` | `{"username": "misty"}` or `{"invite_code": "K7MQ9-XWD3H"}`. `status` is `pending`, or `accepted` if they had asked you first. |
+| POST | `/requests/{id}/accept` | Only the person who was asked. |
+| POST | `/requests/{id}/decline` | Only the person who was asked. |
+| DELETE | `/requests/{id}` | Take back your own unanswered request. |
+| DELETE | `/{user_id}` | End a friendship. |
+| GET | `/trade-list` | Your collection rows marked For Trade: `collection_item_id`, `quantity`, `owned`. |
+| PUT | `/trade-list/{collection_item_id}` | `{"quantity": 2}`; 0 takes the row off. At most the copies you hold. |
+| DELETE | `/trade-list/{collection_item_id}` | Same as quantity 0. |
+| GET | `/{user_id}/profile` | Who they are to you and which sections you may open. |
+| GET | `/{user_id}/collection` | Their shared collection. `for_trade_quantity` is `null` unless their For Trade list is shared with you too. |
+| GET | `/{user_id}/wishlist` | Their shared wishlist. |
+| GET | `/{user_id}/trade-list` | Their For Trade cards. |
+| GET | `/{user_id}/trade-match` | See above. |
+
+Cards in every response have the shape of the catalogue card the rest of the API returns (`CardWithSet`), with its prices:
+the catalogue and its pricing are shared, nothing is copied.
+
+## Storage
+
+Three new tables, created on start-up; no existing table or column is changed:
+
+* `friend_settings` - one row per user that has opened Friends: the three sharing levels (default `private`) and the invite code
+* `friend_links` - one row per pair of users: a request waiting, or an accepted friendship
+* `trade_list` - copies of a collection row marked For Trade
+
+Deleting a user removes their rows. Removing a collection row removes its For Trade mark.
